Actually, on windowed/tiled Nethack, popup windows already do exists for  
certain messages (eg the Quest dialog).
And, iirc (not played in a long time), these windows don't disappear by a  
keystroke but need you to click on them which is sometimes slightly  
annoying but ensure that you won't miss the text.

(I've seen this both on Debian and Mac)


However, messages such as being weak/fainting/being low on HP might become  
really really annoying if they keep poping.
Like "Yes, I bloody know I'm weak, I'm trying to hunt something and living  
on apples in the meantimes, just let me play and kill that jackal !"
Or, similar "Yeah, I'm low on HP, it's not as if I was trying to outrun a  
mob of arrow-shooting orcs. Please let me move!"

I had also many deaths by lack of reading the messages and status bar, I  
guess everybody had while learning the game, and I still have some of them  
when I play. I'm playing with tiles and didn't managed to apply the  
statuscolor/menucolor patches because I guess there're do much related to  
the interface itself (a part of the code I certainly don't want to dig in).
At some point, I considered the idea of having popup windows when HP was  
low (eg <1/4 of total) but then realised that this might be usefull in the  
mid to end game (I've lost many 100+ HP combat wombat in summoning storm  
because they could dispatch basically anything in a couple of blow and  
would not thing of running away...) However, this would be more than  
annoying in the early game where you're like always going up and down the  
1/4 total HP limit and you know (or at least you should know) that with  
15- total HP being carreful is the only key to survival.

I remember a bug in one of the version of Slash'em I played where a popup  
window appeared when you walked on a tile with exactly 2 objects on it.  
And the window was only dismissable by clicking on it which was just  
bloody annoying when going through the same path several times (Sokoban,  
typically). And I'm afraid that the number of time where I actually miss  
the critical message/status information is small compared to the number of  
times I don't miss it, so that a systematic popup windows will annoy me  
more than help me.

However, I guess what you suggest might be a simple modification of the  
code since it might be simply a change of method in a couple of places.

